TEST 18948 -- VRRP routes near wirespeed multple instances
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-10-01 08:14-08:27 NZDT, tb470, bench-runner (queue CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md
        row 12, sentinel device-testing-67). Hand-driven: no framework script.
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 (stack and IE520-sa)
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, master member 3), router MAC
        0000.cd37.0d6f. VRRP peer (backup): the standalone IE520-sa (84e3.2787.0780).
Case:   AWPTCM-T18948 (ck.db), objective "IP is routed at near wirespeed with multiple VRRP
        instances and multiple vlans". One step: "IP is routed at wirespeed when VRRP enabled.
        Multiple instances & multiple VLANs." -> expected "Near wirespeed achieved."
        The case gives no diagram. The scenario here: four VRRP instances in four VLANs, the
        DUT as master and the SA as backup on each, and hosts in all four VLANs sending
        through their VRRP virtual router at once.

VERDICT: PASS.
  Four VRRP instances (VRID 81-84 on vlan3181-3184) formed with the DUT Master and the SA
  Backup on each. VMAC was enabled, and the virtual MACs 0000.5e00.0151-0154 were installed
  to the CPU on the DUT. Traffic from hosts in all four VLANs, addressed to the four virtual
  MACs, was routed by the DUT at line rate with nothing lost:
    1518 B tagged (1514 B routed), 30 s: 984.03 Mbps, 81,031 pps = 99.95 % of the 81,070 pps
      1G maximum; sent 2,431,001, received 2,431,001 (100.0000 %), 607,750-607,751 per VLAN.
    68 B tagged (64 B routed), 30 s: 261,281 pps, 7,839,001 / 7,839,001 (100.0000 %), even
      per VLAN. That rate is the tcpreplay ceiling (~18 % of the 1.42 Mpps small-frame
      maximum), not the DUT's.
  Controls:
    - The same frames with no VRRP configured delivered 0 of 811,001.
    - Received frames are routed: TTL 64 -> 63, source MAC = the DUT's router MAC.
  VRRP held through the load: no Master transition on either router, and the backup
  received one advertisement per second per instance with none missing (see step 3).

